<h1>What Cloudflare is and how the edge works</h1>
<p class="lede">Cloudflare runs one of the world's largest networks of data centres. Websites put Cloudflare in front of them for speed and protection, and developers run code on it. Here is what that means, from zero.</p>

<h2>The internet in one paragraph</h2>
<p>Your computer and a website's computer (a <em>server</em>) talk by sending small packets across many networks. Distance matters: a packet from Mumbai to a server in Virginia and back takes about 200 milliseconds just travelling. A page that needs ten round trips takes two seconds before any work is done.</p>

<h2>What Cloudflare adds</h2>
<div class="callout eli5"><span class="callout-title">Explained simply</span>Instead of one shop in a faraway city, imagine a chain with a branch in every neighbourhood. You walk to the nearest one. Small things (bread, milk = static files) are on the shelf there. For special orders, the local branch can cook it on the spot (run your code), and it has a pantry (database) it can reach.</div>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The edge</strong>: hundreds of data centres in cities around the world. Your request goes to the nearest one, chosen automatically by a routing technique called Anycast (many locations share the same IP address).</li>
  <li><strong>CDN</strong> (content delivery network): static files are kept close to users.</li>
  <li><strong>Protection</strong>: DDoS mitigation, TLS (the padlock), firewall rules.</li>
  <li><strong>Developer platform</strong>: <em>Workers</em> (your code), <em>D1</em> (database), KV, R2 (file storage), Queues, Durable Objects, cron triggers, and more.</li>
</ul>

